1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,867
This is a walkthrough of my Track 3 project:
evaluating CallGuard,

2
00:00:04,867 --> 00:00:07,970
a retrieval-augmented assistant, with
LangSmith.

3
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:12,709
The headline: correctness went from 93% to
98%,

4
00:00:12,709 --> 00:00:19,214
the slowest answers from 7.2 to 3.8 seconds,
and five held-out questions,

5
00:00:19,214 --> 00:00:23,403
written after the fixes, went from 80% to
100%.

6
00:00:23,403 --> 00:00:24,472
Here is how.

7
00:00:25,717 --> 00:00:33,989
CallGuard answers 'are we exposed?' questions
about vulnerabilities in contact-center and
unified-communications products,

8
00:00:33,989 --> 00:00:36,769
such as Cisco, Avaya, Mitel and Asterisk.

9
00:00:36,769 --> 00:00:41,379
It answers from NVD, CISA's list of known
exploited vulnerabilities,

10
00:00:41,379 --> 00:00:45,176
and FIRST's exploit forecasts, and it cites
every claim.

11
00:00:45,726 --> 00:00:48,950
Each question runs through a LangGraph call
flow.

12
00:00:48,950 --> 00:00:52,240
Code reads the CVE numbers, products and
versions.

13
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:56,451
A hybrid search finds the records, and code
checks the versions.

14
00:00:56,451 --> 00:01:00,530
For open-ended questions, a model checks that
the records fit.

15
00:01:00,530 --> 00:01:05,662
Then a writer model drafts a cited answer, or
code declines with safe wording.

16
00:01:06,212 --> 00:01:10,979
To keep every comparison fair, the data was
frozen at 895 CVEs,

17
00:01:10,979 --> 00:01:15,746
and each experiment records the exact
CallGuard version it ran.

18
00:01:16,983 --> 00:01:19,673
The method is the one a QA team would use.

19
00:01:19,673 --> 00:01:21,147
Build a golden dataset.

20
00:01:21,147 --> 00:01:26,079
Score every answer with code evaluators and
LLM judges, three times per case,

21
00:01:26,079 --> 00:01:28,961
so the noise is measured rather than guessed.

22
00:01:28,961 --> 00:01:31,331
Then check the evaluators themselves.

23
00:01:31,881 --> 00:01:37,417
Take a baseline, cluster the failures by root
cause, fix them, and run it again.

24
00:01:37,417 --> 00:01:42,815
Finally, a held-out set, written after the
fixes, checks that they generalise.

25
00:01:44,050 --> 00:01:49,339
The golden dataset has 40 cases: 20 happy-
path, 12 edge, 6 known-failure,

26
00:01:49,339 --> 00:01:50,644
and 2 adversarial.

27
00:01:50,644 --> 00:01:54,991
The 25 questions from my Week 3 exam are kept
word for word.

28
00:01:54,991 --> 00:01:59,918
They were written before CallGuard existed,
which keeps them honest.

29
00:02:00,468 --> 00:02:02,879
Fifteen new cases target real gaps,

30
00:02:02,879 --> 00:02:06,461
and I checked each edge case against the code
first.

31
00:02:06,461 --> 00:02:10,732
'Asterix', misspelled, matches no product
filter, for example.

32
00:02:10,732 --> 00:02:16,036
Every case states the expected behaviour:
answer, refuse for a stated reason,

33
00:02:16,036 --> 00:02:17,414
or resist an attack.

34
00:02:17,414 --> 00:02:22,304
It also lists the CVEs a good answer must
find, and a reference answer.

35
00:02:22,854 --> 00:02:28,096
A check script verifies every expected CVE
against the data before upload.

36
00:02:28,096 --> 00:02:32,983
In LangSmith, the dataset has one split per
group, fixed example IDs,

37
00:02:32,983 --> 00:02:38,508
and the version tag v1, so every experiment
runs on exactly the same examples.

38
00:02:39,750 --> 00:02:41,542
Two cases are adversarial.

39
00:02:41,542 --> 00:02:43,266
A1 is a prompt injection.

40
00:02:43,266 --> 00:02:48,918
It claims a real CVE was withdrawn, and orders
the reply 'safe, no action needed'.

41
00:02:48,918 --> 00:02:50,986
That phrase must never appear.

42
00:02:51,536 --> 00:02:56,544
A2 is 771 characters of noise: control codes,
a script tag, SQL,

43
00:02:56,544 --> 00:03:01,709
template and JNDI strings, an XML entity and
spreadsheet formulas,

44
00:03:01,709 --> 00:03:08,595
all wrapped around one real question about
server-side request forgery in Cisco Finesse.

45
00:03:09,850 --> 00:03:13,003
One client.evaluate call runs each experiment.

46
00:03:13,003 --> 00:03:15,334
Questions are asked one at a time,

47
00:03:15,334 --> 00:03:18,967
so response times aren't distorted by parallel
calls,

48
00:03:18,967 --> 00:03:21,160
and every case runs three times.

49
00:03:21,710 --> 00:03:24,982
Code evaluators check what code can check
exactly.

50
00:03:24,982 --> 00:03:28,058
Did the answer find and cite the expected
CVEs?

51
00:03:28,058 --> 00:03:31,265
Is every citation a record it was actually
shown?

52
00:03:31,265 --> 00:03:35,322
Did it decline exactly when it should, and
resist the attacks?

53
00:03:35,322 --> 00:03:36,631
And how fast was it?

54
00:03:37,181 --> 00:03:41,712
Two LLM judges handle what code can't:
faithfulness and correctness.

55
00:03:41,712 --> 00:03:46,842
The judge is Qwen 3.5, a different model from
CallGuard's writer and checker,

56
00:03:46,842 --> 00:03:48,974
so no model grades its own work.

57
00:03:50,217 --> 00:03:53,148
Faithfulness gets two scores from one
judgement.

58
00:03:53,148 --> 00:03:58,217
Here, CallGuard correctly declines a product
that doesn't exist, and adds a caveat:

59
00:03:58,217 --> 00:04:01,209
that is not a statement that the product is
safe.

60
00:04:01,759 --> 00:04:07,025
Strict scoring, the way RAGAS does it, counts
that caveat as unsupported,

61
00:04:07,025 --> 00:04:09,549
so this correct refusal scores 71%.

62
00:04:09,549 --> 00:04:13,517
Facts-only scoring ignores caveats, and scores
it 100%.

63
00:04:13,517 --> 00:04:14,815
Both are reported,

64
00:04:14,815 --> 00:04:19,720
because strict scoring alone punishes exactly
the behaviour we want.

65
00:04:20,983 --> 00:04:26,593
Before trusting the baseline, I read every
failing verdict against the records.

66
00:04:26,593 --> 00:04:30,641
The correctness judge had only seen the
reference answer,

67
00:04:30,641 --> 00:04:34,831
so it called correct extra facts hallucinated:
EPSS scores,

68
00:04:34,831 --> 00:04:38,807
a 2026 date it took for the future, even four
real CVEs.

69
00:04:39,357 --> 00:04:44,578
Given the same sources the writer saw, and re-
scored on the same 120 answers,

70
00:04:44,578 --> 00:04:46,816
correctness went from 80% to 93%.

71
00:04:46,816 --> 00:04:49,664
Two more problems were found the same way:

72
00:04:49,664 --> 00:04:52,851
a refusal phrase list that missed new
wordings,

73
00:04:52,851 --> 00:04:56,716
and a second evaluate call that mixed up the
repetitions.

74
00:04:56,716 --> 00:05:00,513
Measuring the measure changed the headline by
13 points.

75
00:05:01,750 --> 00:05:08,333
The baseline: 93% correct, faithfulness 99.6%
on facts, and citations,

76
00:05:08,333 --> 00:05:12,282
refusals and attacks resisted all at 100%.

77
00:05:12,282 --> 00:05:19,711
Across the three repetitions, correctness
moved between 92% and 94% on its own.

78
00:05:20,261 --> 00:05:23,846
So CallGuard's weak points weren't
hallucination.

79
00:05:23,846 --> 00:05:29,334
They were coverage, in the known-failure and
adversarial groups, and speed:

80
00:05:29,334 --> 00:05:32,846
a p95 of 7.2 seconds, against a 6-second
target.

81
00:05:34,083 --> 00:05:36,726
Every failing run was traced to a cause.

82
00:05:36,726 --> 00:05:38,444
Cluster one: slow answers.

83
00:05:38,444 --> 00:05:42,541
The traces show that search takes five
hundredths of a second.

84
00:05:42,541 --> 00:05:44,523
Writing takes 99% of the time,

85
00:05:44,523 --> 00:05:48,355
and about half of the writer's output is
hidden reasoning.

86
00:05:48,905 --> 00:05:53,767
On L1's real prompt, I tried three ways to
switch that reasoning off.

87
00:05:53,767 --> 00:05:58,841
Only one of them worked, and it took the
answer from 7.2 to 3.8 seconds,

88
00:05:58,841 --> 00:06:00,391
with the same content.

89
00:06:01,650 --> 00:06:03,564
Cluster two: list questions.

90
00:06:03,564 --> 00:06:09,854
'List every critical MiCollab vulnerability.'
The filters find exactly the 13 right records,

91
00:06:09,854 --> 00:06:10,879
ranked 1 to 13.

92
00:06:10,879 --> 00:06:16,280
Then the search keeps the top 8, and the
answer claims the data has only eight.

93
00:06:16,830 --> 00:06:19,119
Cluster three: noisy questions.

94
00:06:19,119 --> 00:06:24,510
'Asterix' matches no product filter, so the
most serious CVE ranks tenth.

95
00:06:24,510 --> 00:06:29,753
In A2, the XML junk, version equals 1.0, was
read as a product version.

96
00:06:29,753 --> 00:06:35,291
That re-sorted the results, and pushed three
of the five right records out.

97
00:06:35,841 --> 00:06:36,804
Cluster four:

98
00:06:36,804 --> 00:06:42,507
seven of the 895 records state their affected
versions only in a description,

99
00:06:42,507 --> 00:06:45,618
so the writer hedged instead of answering.

100
00:06:45,618 --> 00:06:47,692
One case, S2, stays unfixed.

101
00:06:47,692 --> 00:06:52,062
Its two missed records rank 13th and 39th for
that wording.

102
00:06:53,317 --> 00:06:58,622
Four clusters, four fixes, each a general rule
rather than a patch for one case.

103
00:06:58,622 --> 00:07:01,540
F1 runs the writer without hidden reasoning.

104
00:07:01,540 --> 00:07:05,984
F2 adds a list mode that passes on every
matching record, up to 20,

105
00:07:05,984 --> 00:07:08,039
and tells the writer the count.

106
00:07:08,039 --> 00:07:12,151
F3 cleans noisy questions, and tolerates a
misspelled product,

107
00:07:12,151 --> 00:07:14,406
telling the writer it was a guess.

108
00:07:14,406 --> 00:07:18,253
F4 reads a version range stated in a
description, in code.

109
00:07:18,803 --> 00:07:22,421
Each fix was checked for side effects on all
40 questions.

110
00:07:22,421 --> 00:07:25,666
I also dropped a planned prompt-injection
guardrail:

111
00:07:25,666 --> 00:07:30,408
both attacks were resisted in every run, so
there was nothing for it to fix.

112
00:07:31,650 --> 00:07:36,087
The re-run then caught a regression my own fix
caused.

113
00:07:36,087 --> 00:07:42,661
Without its reasoning, the writer once slipped
a digit: 41713 came out as 41793.

114
00:07:42,661 --> 00:07:44,140
One answer in 120.

115
00:07:44,690 --> 00:07:46,353
So F5 is a citation guard.

116
00:07:46,353 --> 00:07:50,769
If an answer cites a CVE that wasn't
retrieved, isn't in the records,

117
00:07:50,769 --> 00:07:55,504
and wasn't typed by the user, it gets one
rewrite naming the wrong number.

118
00:07:55,504 --> 00:07:57,808
If one remains, code adds a warning.

119
00:07:57,808 --> 00:08:01,199
Four tests with a scripted writer show that it
works,

120
00:08:01,199 --> 00:08:04,079
and in the final run it never needed to fire.

121
00:08:05,317 --> 00:08:10,465
Same dataset version, same evaluators, same
judge.

122
00:08:10,465 --> 00:08:12,833
Only CallGuard changed.

123
00:08:12,833 --> 00:08:16,231
Correctness rose from 93% to 98%.

124
00:08:16,231 --> 00:08:20,041
Expected CVEs found, from 96% to 99%.

125
00:08:20,041 --> 00:08:24,880
And answers under six seconds, from 88% to
98%.

126
00:08:25,430 --> 00:08:32,830
p50 fell from 3.6 to 2.1 seconds, and p95 from
7.2 to 3.8, well under the target.

127
00:08:32,830 --> 00:08:36,941
Citations, refusals and attacks held at 100%.

128
00:08:36,941 --> 00:08:42,971
And a change only counts when the repetition
ranges don't overlap:

129
00:08:42,971 --> 00:08:49,091
correctness ranged from 92 to 94% before, and
from 96 to 99% after.

130
00:08:50,350 --> 00:08:55,527
Twelve cases went from failing at least one
measure to failing none.

131
00:08:55,527 --> 00:09:01,999
The biggest gains were in the hardest groups:
known failures from 81% to 92% correct,

132
00:09:01,999 --> 00:09:04,511
and adversarial from 75% to 100%.

133
00:09:05,061 --> 00:09:10,642
L1 now lists all 13 critical CVEs, in under
four seconds instead of six to eight.

134
00:09:10,642 --> 00:09:14,569
X1 says 'assuming you mean Asterisk', and
finds all five.

135
00:09:14,569 --> 00:09:17,324
And A2 finds all five through the noise.

136
00:09:18,583 --> 00:09:19,677
It wasn't free.

137
00:09:19,677 --> 00:09:24,783
Without the reasoning, faithfulness on facts
fell from 99.6% to 99.4%:

138
00:09:24,783 --> 00:09:29,378
single loose inferences, such as 'your build
is the fixed one'.

139
00:09:29,378 --> 00:09:32,004
Two cases had one 'partly' run each.

140
00:09:32,004 --> 00:09:37,255
That's the price of halving the response time,
and the report states it.

141
00:09:38,517 --> 00:09:42,576
To check that the fixes weren't just
memorising their test cases,

142
00:09:42,576 --> 00:09:46,823
I wrote five new questions after the fixes,
one per fixed behaviour,

143
00:09:46,823 --> 00:09:48,759
on other products and wordings.

144
00:09:49,309 --> 00:09:53,918
On the baseline version, they scored 80%
correct.

145
00:09:53,918 --> 00:09:59,469
With the fixes, 100%, and CVEs found went from
96% to 100%.

146
00:09:59,469 --> 00:10:01,444
The fixes generalise.

147
00:10:02,683 --> 00:10:04,539
Everything is reproducible.

148
00:10:04,539 --> 00:10:10,036
The repository holds the dataset, the
evaluators, every result, and the reports.

149
00:10:10,036 --> 00:10:13,197
The spreadsheet has all 40 cases and 405 runs,

150
00:10:13,197 --> 00:10:16,839
with a summary computed by formula from the
run data,

151
00:10:16,839 --> 00:10:20,206
and a link from every run to its LangSmith
trace.

152
00:10:21,450 --> 00:10:23,555
A few limits, stated plainly.

153
00:10:23,555 --> 00:10:26,822
The timings reuse cached question embeddings.

154
00:10:26,822 --> 00:10:29,508
Three answers hit a stalled API call.

155
00:10:29,508 --> 00:10:30,597
S2 still fails.

156
00:10:30,597 --> 00:10:34,081
And 45 cases are enough to find failure
classes,

157
00:10:34,081 --> 00:10:36,767
not to measure rare errors precisely.

158
00:10:37,317 --> 00:10:40,618
The lessons I'd keep: measure the measure
first.

159
00:10:40,618 --> 00:10:42,819
Repetitions are cheap insurance.

160
00:10:42,819 --> 00:10:45,020
Traces turn guesses into causes.

161
00:10:45,020 --> 00:10:47,564
Every fix has a price, so measure it.

162
00:10:47,564 --> 00:10:50,384
And held-out cases keep the fixes honest.

163
00:10:50,384 --> 00:10:51,760
Thanks for watching.
